# 01B — First Playable: Refinements

> A running log of refinements made after `01-first-playable` reached its goal. The loop works: a human draws squares in the editor, and a Claude Code agent reads and draws squares over MCP, live. Each entry records what changed, why, and any decision it settled. New refinements go at the bottom.

---

## R1 — Full-window canvas and a floating bottom bar

**Asked for:**
- The canvas fills the whole window.
- Resizing the window is handled.
- Tools and state live in a floating bar at the bottom, with a margin.

**What changed:**
- **Full-window canvas.** The SVG fills the viewport (`position: fixed; inset: 0`), and the old top header is gone. Scale stays at 1 u = 20 px, and world `0,0` is the window's top-left corner.
- **Resize.** The editor measures the window (a `resize` listener) and derives its visible world area from it: `width = innerWidth / 20`, `height = innerHeight / 20`. The grid re-renders to cover the new area. Squares don't move, because resizing changes only how much of the world you see, never the data.
- **Floating bar.** The bar is centered at the bottom, 16 px from the edges, with a translucent dark pill, rounded corners and a shadow. It holds:
  - the legend (human/agent colors) and the square count
  - the drawing hint (drag to draw, Alt for free placement)
  - the cursor readout in world units (it moved here from the corner overlay)
  - the connection status and the Clear button

  Errors show as a toast just above the bar. The bar wraps on narrow windows.
- **The view follows the window.** The editor reports its visible area to the server (new WS message `set_view`) on connect and on every resize. `get_scene` therefore returns the area the human can actually see, so "draw 10 squares" lands on screen at any window size. Before this, `view` was a fixed 60×40 u that no longer matched a full-window canvas.

**Decisions:**
- `view` in the scene is now "last reported editor viewport", not a fixed drawing area. If several tabs are open, the last one to report wins. That's fine for a single-user tool, and we'll revisit it if it causes confusion. Setting the view is not an edit: it doesn't broadcast and won't enter undo history later.
- The default view (60×40 u) only applies before any editor has connected.
- The bar is drawn over the canvas and can hide the squares behind it. We accept this for now. Pan (a future phase) is the real fix.

**Files:**
- `src/web/App.tsx`: `useWindowView`, full-window SVG, and the dock/bar
- `src/web/styles.css`: rewritten for the full-window layout and the floating bar
- `src/shared/scene.types.ts`: `ViewSchema` and the `set_view` message
- `src/server/scene.ts`: `setView`
- `src/server/ws.ts`: handles `set_view`
- `src/server/mcp.ts`: tool descriptions now describe `view` as the visible window
- `CLAUDE.md`: updated `view` description

**Verified:** typecheck, and unit tests (7, including `setView` not broadcasting). A scripted check showed a `set_view` over WS is reflected in MCP `get_scene`. Browser layout and resize are checked by hand in the browser.

